  [1mMy recommendation: console + ring buffer.[0m It gives you the pre-failure history that makes production bugs debuggable,
  needs no backend this repo doesn't have, and the buffer is exactly the seam a collector would plug into later if you
  decide you want one.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should browser logs go? → Console + ring buffer (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Console + ring buffer. Noting that the buffer changes the stakes of the next question: entries are retained and meant
  to be dumped and shared, so anything that lands in them travels.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what the logger does when a call site hands it something that shouldn't be recorded. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m already logs the
  username, and [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m takes a password one scope away from a log call — this is a live hazard, not
  a hypothetical.

  - [1mKey-name denylist in the logger[0m — the logger walks logged objects and masks values under keys matching [38;5;153mpassword[39m,
    [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauth[39m, etc. Buys protection at every call site, including ones written later by someone who didn't
    read the convention. Costs a shallow object walk and a key list to maintain. Cheap to tighten later.
  - [1mCaller's responsibility (convention only)[0m — logger records what it's given; a comment says don't log secrets. Zero
    logger code. Failure mode is a password sitting in a buffer that's designed to be exported.
  - [1mField allowlist[0m — call sites declare which fields are safe; everything else is dropped. Strongest guarantee, most
    friction, and it makes the logger annoying enough that people route around it with [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m.

  [1mMy recommendation: key-name denylist.[0m It's the only one of the three that survives the call site you haven't written
  yet, and the cost is a dozen lines.
